Subject: [PATCH v2] claude: Treat unintegrated mail as revisable drafts
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Change-Id: ysonztpozwrplkkvxtwsylsmnkmpksuu

The outbox was write-once: a reply, once pushed to submissions, stayed
as written even when a later message or a change on trunk made it
wrong. Pat then reviewed mail that its own author already knew was out
of date, and corrections could only follow as more mail.

Until the moderator integrates it, nobody has built on a message, so
rewriting it costs nothing. process-email now checks its unintegrated
mail before each pass and revises or drops what new information has
overtaken.

A patch series is the exception. Review and later versions refer to a
series by its version, so rewriting one in place leaves them pointing
at a series nobody can see. A series that needs to change goes out as
its next version instead.

The moderator may be reading a draft while it is rewritten, and may
integrate the original. A message on the list is immutable, so when a
revision conflicts with it, the list wins and anything still worth
saying goes in a new reply.
